<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Vocabulary on posets and lattices</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E|</annotation></semantics></math> the cardinality of a set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. For a positive integer <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The terms <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">smaller</em>, <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bigger</em>, <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">below</em> and <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">above</em> will always refer to weak relations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If a partially ordered set (poset) <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P,\leq)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P,\leq)</annotation></semantics></math> has a minimum, it will always be denoted <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for the maximum. The cover relations of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are denoted <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\lessdot y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\lessdot y</annotation></semantics></math> and we say that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">covers</em> <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">is covered by</em> <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. They form the set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(P)</annotation></semantics></math> of the edges of its Hasse diagram, which is drawn with smaller elements at the bottom. The interval of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x,y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math>.
A subset <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">convex</span> if for all <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x,y]\subseteq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x,y]\subseteq C</annotation></semantics></math>. An <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">order ideal</span> of a poset <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in P</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\leq x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\leq x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in I</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in I</annotation></semantics></math>. The order ideal generated by a subset <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m28" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{P}(C):=\{y\in P\mid\exists x\in C,\,y\leq x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{P}(C):=\{y\in P\mid\exists x\in C,\,y\leq x\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{P}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{P}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Dually, an <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">order filter</span> is a subset <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in P</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\geq x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\geq x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in F</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in F</annotation></semantics></math>. The order filter generated by a subset <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m37" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{P}(C):=\{y\in P\mid\exists x\in C,\,y\geq x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{P}(C):=\{y\in P\mid\exists x\in C,\,y\geq x\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{P}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{P}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">chains</span> are the totally ordered subsets of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A chain <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> elements is said to have length <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, which is denoted by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(F)</annotation></semantics></math>. If we cannot add elements to a chain, it is called a <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">maximal chain</span>. The <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">length</span> of a poset, denoted <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(P)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the maximum length of a chain in <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. The chains of length <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(P)</annotation></semantics></math> are called <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">longest chains</span>. The <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">spine</span> of a poset is the subset of the elements that lie on some longest chain.
If <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> are two posets, then the <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">direct product</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times Q</annotation></semantics></math> is the poset on the cartesian product <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times Q</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\leq(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\leq(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\leq x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\leq y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\leq y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">lattice</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a poset such that any pair of elements <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x,y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x,y\}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a least upper bound, called the <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">join</span> and written <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\vee y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\vee y</annotation></semantics></math>, and a greatest lower bound, called the <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">meet</span> and written <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\wedge y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\wedge y</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">join-irreducible</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in L</annotation></semantics></math> is an element that covers a unique element, denoted <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">meet-irreducible</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in L</annotation></semantics></math> is one that is covered by a unique element, denoted <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The sets of these elements are respectively denoted <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>MIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. An <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">edge-labelling</span> of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a map <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:E(L)\rightarrow P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:E(L)\rightarrow P</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a poset.
We will say that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in L</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">incomparable</span> to a subset <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subseteq L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subseteq L</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is incomparable to any element of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Extremal lattices</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is well known that in a lattice, an element is always the join of the join-irreducibles below it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(L)\leq\min\big(|\mathrm{JIrr}(L)|,|\mathrm{MIrr}(L)|\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>MIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(L)\leq\min\big(|\mathrm{JIrr}(L)|,|\mathrm{MIrr}(L)|\big)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Denote <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\ell(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\ell(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{0}\lessdot c_{1}\lessdot c_{2}\lessdot\dots\lessdot c_{k+1}=\hat{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⋖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{0}\lessdot c_{1}\lessdot c_{2}\lessdot\dots\lessdot c_{k+1}=\hat{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a maximal chain of length <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k-1]</annotation></semantics></math>, there is always a join-irreducible that is below <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> but not <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. If it was not the case, then all the join-irreducibles below <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> would be below <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> which is the join of them would be below <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is absurd. It follows that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq|\mathrm{JIrr}(L)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq|\mathrm{JIrr}(L)|</annotation></semantics></math>. A dual argument gives <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\leq|\mathrm{MIrr}(L)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>MIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\leq|\mathrm{MIrr}(L)|</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The previous result motivated the following definition by G. Markowsky <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">join-extremal</span> if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(L)=|\mathrm{JIrr}(L)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(L)=|\mathrm{JIrr}(L)|</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">meet-extremal</span> if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(L)=~|\mathrm{MIrr}(L)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>MIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(L)=~|\mathrm{MIrr}(L)|</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">extremal</span> if it is both join-extremal and meet-extremal.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Two notable results on these lattices are that the graded extremal lattices are the distributive lattices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, Theorem <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17</annotation></semantics></math>]</cite>, and any lattice is isomorphic to an interval of an extremal lattice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, Theorem <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math>]</cite>. G. Markowsky also proved a representation theorem of extremal lattices, that we now explain with recent terminology (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Section 2.3]</cite>).
For an extremal lattice <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the choice of any longest chain <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\,\hat{0}=x_{0}\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{n}=\hat{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⋖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\,\hat{0}=x_{0}\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{n}=\hat{1}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a numbering of the join and meet-irreducibles <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1},j_{2},\dots,j_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1},j_{2},\dots,j_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}=j_{1}\vee\dots\vee j_{i}=m_{i+1}\wedge\dots\wedge m_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}=j_{1}\vee\dots\vee j_{i}=m_{i+1}\wedge\dots\wedge m_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be an extremal lattice.
We define a directed graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Galois graph</span>, whose vertices are <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> and with an edge <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{i}\rightarrow j_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{i}\rightarrow j_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{i}\not\leq m_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≰</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{i}\not\leq m_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We defined the Galois graph <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> on the set <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> as that will be the way we see it in <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, but with our numbering of join and meet-irreducibles, it can be equivalently seen as the graph on <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math> with directed edges <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow k</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{i}\not\leq m_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≰</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{i}\not\leq m_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, Theorem 11]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any extremal lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the Galois graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a directed graph on <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math> without multiple edges and such that whenever <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow k</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>. A <span id="S2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">maximal orthogonal pair</span> of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a pair <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math> of disjoint subsets of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math> with no directed edges from <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and which is maximal for this condition. With the partial order <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)\leq(X^{\prime},Y^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)\leq(X^{\prime},Y^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the maximal orthogonal pairs form a lattice denoted by <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S4.F9" title="In 4.1. The Hochschild and bubble lattices ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every finite extremal lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G(L))</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a directed graph on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]</annotation></semantics></math> without multiple edges and such that whenever <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow k</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is an extremal lattice.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This result will enable us in <a href="#S4" title="4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> to define quickly an extremal lattice by giving its Galois graph.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Semidistributive lattices</h3>

<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">join-semidistributive</span> if for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y,z\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y,z\in L</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\vee y=x\vee z\Longrightarrow x\vee(y\wedge z)=x\vee y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\vee y=x\vee z\Longrightarrow x\vee(y\wedge z)=x\vee y</annotation></semantics></math>. It is <span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">meet-semidistributive</span> if for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y,z\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y,z\in L</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\wedge y=x\wedge z\Longrightarrow x\wedge(y\vee z)=x\vee y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\wedge y=x\wedge z\Longrightarrow x\wedge(y\vee z)=x\vee y</annotation></semantics></math>. It is <span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semidistributive</span> if it is both join-semidistributive and meet-semidistributive.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following is well-known:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Section 5]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is join-semidistributive if and only if for all covers <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math>, the set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{L}(c)\setminus I_{L}(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{L}(c)\setminus I_{L}(b)</annotation></semantics></math> has a minimum element, denoted <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{J}(b\lessdot c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{J}(b\lessdot c)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is meet-semidistributive if and only if for all covers <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math>, the set <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{L}(b)\setminus F_{L}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{L}(b)\setminus F_{L}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> has a maximum element, denoted <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{M}(b\lessdot c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{M}(b\lessdot c)</annotation></semantics></math>. In these cases, the minimum is join-irreducible and the maximum meet-irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Thus by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7 ([, Section 5]). ‣ 2.3. Semidistributive lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, if <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is semidistributive we have two edge-labellings <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{J}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the map <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa:j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)\mapstochar\rightarrow\gamma_{M}(j_{*}\lessdot j)\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>⋖</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa:j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)\mapstochar\rightarrow\gamma_{M}(j_{*}\lessdot j)\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is a bijection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, Theorem 2.54]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Proposition 6.2]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math> in a semidistributive lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\big(\gamma_{J}(b\lessdot c)\big)=\gamma_{M}(b\lessdot c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\big(\gamma_{J}(b\lessdot c)\big)=\gamma_{M}(b\lessdot c)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall as explained earlier that if <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is extremal, choosing a longest chain of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> gives a numbering of the join-irreducible elements <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1},\dots,j_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1},\dots,j_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the meet-irreducible elements <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},\dots,m_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},\dots,m_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The next statement follows from <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 2.8 ([] and [, Proposition 6.2]). ‣ 2.3. Semidistributive lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lat <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a semidistributive extremal lattice. We have <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(j_{i})=m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(j_{i})=m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the Galois graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is the directed graph whose vertices are <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> and there is an edge <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\not\leq\kappa(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≰</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\not\leq\kappa(j)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a semidistributive lattice. For any <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in L</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D(x):=\{\gamma_{J}(y\lessdot x)\mid y\in~L,\,y\lessdot x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.608em">∈</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x):=\{\gamma_{J}(y\lessdot x)\mid y\in~L,\,y\lessdot x\}</annotation></semantics></math> and call it the <span id="S2.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">downward label set</span> of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. The canonical join complex is often defined using the notion of canonical join representations, but the following definition is equivalent:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">canonical join complex</span> of a semidistributive lattice is the set of the downward label sets.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is more generally defined for join-semidistributive lattices, but in this article we will always assume that the canonical join complexes come from a semidistributive lattice.
The name is justified by the fact that it is a simplicial complex (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, Proposition <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.2</annotation></semantics></math>]</cite>). The <span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">clique complex</span> of a graph <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the simplicial complex whose faces are the cliques of <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
The canonical join complex is flag (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Theorem 1]</cite>), which by definition means that it is the clique complex of its 1-skeleton. This 1-skeleton is called the <span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">canonical join graph</span> of the lattice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">complement</span> of a (directed) graph <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the simple undirected graph on the same vertices where we have an edge between two vertices if there are no (directed) edges between the two in <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the cliques of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}</annotation></semantics></math> are the independence sets of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Corollary 6.7]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The canonical join graph of a semidistributive extremal lattice is the complement of its Galois graph.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Left modular lattices</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The left modular lattices were first defined and studied by A. Blass and B. Sagan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An element <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in L</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">left modular</span> if for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;c</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b\vee a)\wedge c=b\vee(a\wedge c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b\vee a)\wedge c=b\vee(a\wedge c)</annotation></semantics></math> (we gave an equivalent definition in the introduction, which is part of the next result).
A maximal chain made of left modular elements is called a <span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">maximal left modular chain</span>. The lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is called left modular if there exists a maximal left modular chain.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in L</annotation></semantics></math> be an element of a lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. The following statements are equivalent :</span></p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The element </span><math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is left modular.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;c</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, we have </span><math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\vee b\neq a\vee c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\vee b\neq a\vee c</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\wedge b\neq a\wedge c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\wedge b\neq a\wedge c</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all </span><math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, we have </span><math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\vee b\neq a\vee c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\vee b\neq a\vee c</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\wedge b\neq a\wedge c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\wedge b\neq a\wedge c</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I1.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is no pentagonal sublattice </span><math id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{5}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the middle element on the smaller maximal chain of </span><math id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{5}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math> is such that <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem13" title="Proposition 2.13 ([]). ‣ 2.4. Left modular lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a> is not satisfied for <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, we will say that <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">counterexample to the left modularity</em> of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">H. Thomas defined a lattice to be <span id="S2.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">trim</span> if it is extremal and left modular <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>. Trim lattices form a class of lattices, closed under taking intervals and quotients, that include in particular the Tamari lattices and more generally the finite Cambrian lattices.
With N. Williams, they obtained additional results on them, including the fact that extremal semidistributive lattices are trim <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite>. We will give a new proof of this result in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5. </span>Congruence normal lattices</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will use the following doubling construction of A. Day <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.14</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a convex subset of a lattice <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the chain on two elements with order <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the <span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">doubling</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> to be the subposet of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\times\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\times\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> on the subset</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Big(I_{L}(C)\times\{0\}\Big)\,\bigsqcup\,\Big[\Big(\big(L\setminus I_{L}(C)\big)\cup C\Big)\times\{1\}\Big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Big(I_{L}(C)\times\{0\}\Big)\,\bigsqcup\,\Big[\Big(\big(L\setminus I_{L}(C)\big)\cup C\Big)\times\{1\}\Big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In fact <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> is a lattice with meet and join given for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,\epsilon),(y,\epsilon^{\prime})\in L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,\epsilon),(y,\epsilon^{\prime})\in L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.EGx1.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.EGx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(x,\epsilon)\vee(y,\epsilon^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(x,\epsilon)\vee(y,\epsilon^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
