</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We have a surjective poset morphism <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]\rightarrow L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]\rightarrow L</annotation></semantics></math> that sends <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and an injective poset morphism <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:L\rightarrow L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:L\rightarrow L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> that sends <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in L</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,0)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in I_{L}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in I_{L}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> or to <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,1)</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
If <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximal chain of <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">goes through the doubling</em> of <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in C</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(c,0),(c,1)\}\subseteq F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(c,0),(c,1)\}\subseteq F</annotation></semantics></math>.
See <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for examples of the doubling construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From the definition of the doubling construction, the next two results follow.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.15</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Lemma 1]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The join-irreducible elements of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> are of two types; each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,\epsilon)\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L[C])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,\epsilon)\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L[C])</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in I_{L}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in I_{L}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=1</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise, and we also get <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,1)\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L[C])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,1)\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L[C])</annotation></semantics></math> for any minimal element <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Similarly the meet-irreducible elements of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> are of two types; each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,\epsilon)\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L[C])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,\epsilon)\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L[C])</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in I_{L}(C)\setminus C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in I_{L}(C)\setminus C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=1</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise, and we also get <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,0)\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L[C])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,0)\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L[C])</annotation></semantics></math> for any maximal element <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.16</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(L[C])=\ell(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(L[C])=\ell(L)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> does not intersect the spine of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S2.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(L[C])=\ell(L)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(L[C])=\ell(L)+1</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A subset <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S2.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">lower pseudo-interval</span> if <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a union of intervals sharing the same minimum element. It is an <span id="S2.SS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">upper pseudo-interval</span> if it is a union of intervals sharing the same maximum element. A lattice <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">congruence normal</span> if it is obtained from the one element lattice by successive doublings of convex subsets. We write <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=E[C_{1},C_{2},\dots,C_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=E[C_{1},C_{2},\dots,C_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> for the lattice obtained from the one element lattice by doubling successively <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2},\dots,C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2},\dots,C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which means <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i+1}]=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}][C_{i+1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i+1}]=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}][C_{i+1}]</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a convex subset of <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[\,]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[\,]</annotation></semantics></math> is the one element lattice. We will always assume that <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a lower pseudo-interval for all <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS5.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">join-congruence uniform</span>. If all the <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are upper pseudo-intervals then <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS5.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">meet-congruence uniform</span>. If all the <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are intervals, <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S2.SS5.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">congruence uniform<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <em id="footnote1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_medium ltx_font_italic">Join-congruence uniform</em><span id="footnote1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> and </span><em id="footnote1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_medium ltx_font_italic">meet-congruence uniform</em><span id="footnote1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> lattices are often called respectively </span><em id="footnote1.14" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_medium ltx_font_italic">lower-bounded</em><span id="footnote1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> and </span><em id="footnote1.16" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_medium ltx_font_italic">upper-bounded</em><span id="footnote1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> in the literature, but it conflicts with the term </span><em id="footnote1.18" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_medium ltx_font_italic">bounded</em><span id="footnote1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> poset which usually refers to a poset with a minimum and a maximum.</span></span></span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.17</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem17.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A join-congruence uniform lattice is join-semidistributive. A meet-congruence uniform lattice is meet-semidistributive. A congruence uniform lattice is semidistributive.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6. </span>Shellability</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We refer the reader to the papers of A. Björner and M. Wachs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> for details related to the following topological notions. The <span id="S2.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">order complex</span> <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(P)</annotation></semantics></math> of a poset <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is the simplicial complex on vertex set <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> whose faces are the chains of <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the facets of <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(L)</annotation></semantics></math> are the maximal chains of <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S2.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dimension</em> of a face <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(F):=|F|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(F):=|F|-1</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;F&gt;:=\{G\subseteq\Delta(L)\mid G\subseteq F\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;F&gt;:=\{G\subseteq\Delta(L)\mid G\subseteq F\}</annotation></semantics></math> the simplex generated by <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. A simplicial complex is <span id="S2.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">shellable</span> if there exists a linear order of its facets <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1},F_{2},\dots,F_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1},F_{2},\dots,F_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j&lt;k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j&lt;k</annotation></semantics></math>, the complex</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\big(\bigcup_{i=1}^{j}&lt;F_{i}&gt;\big)\bigcap&lt;F_{j+1}&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>j</mi></munderover></mstyle><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">&gt;</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">⋂</mo></mstyle><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\big(\bigcup_{i=1}^{j}&lt;F_{i}&gt;\big)\bigcap&lt;F_{j+1}&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is pure of dimension <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(F_{j+1})-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(F_{j+1})-1</annotation></semantics></math>. We call such a linear order on the facets a <span id="S2.SS6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">shelling</span> of <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1},F_{2},\dots,F_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1},F_{2},\dots,F_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a shelling if and only if for all <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}\,\cap F_{j}\subseteq F_{l}\,\cap F_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.392em">∩</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}\,\cap F_{j}\subseteq F_{l}\,\cap F_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|F_{l}\,\cap F_{j}|=|F_{j}|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|F_{l}\,\cap F_{j}|=|F_{j}|-1</annotation></semantics></math>. We will say that <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is shellable if <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is shellable. It is an easy fact that the first simplex of a shelling is always of longest length. If we have an edge-labelling, a maximal chain gives a word by concatenating the labels of the edges we see by following the chain from bottom to top. In this context we call a maximal chain <em id="S2.SS6.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">increasing</em> if the associated word is increasing.
An <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EL</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS6.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-labelling</span> of a lattice <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge-labelling such that in any interval, when reading the labels following the chains from bottom to top, there is a unique maximal increasing chain and the label word of the increasing chain lexicographically precedes the label word of any other maximal chains. Denote <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{L}:=L\setminus\{\hat{0},\hat{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{L}:=L\setminus\{\hat{0},\hat{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> admits an <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EL</annotation></semantics></math>-labelling, then the order complex <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\overline{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\overline{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is shellable and homotopy equivalent to a wedge of spheres.
Any left modular lattice admits an <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EL</annotation></semantics></math>-labelling (see <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Remark 3.6. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Remark</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>). For any maximal left modular chain, there exists a shelling of <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(L)</annotation></semantics></math> that starts with this chain, thus the left modular chains are always of longest length. It follows from the fact that all semidistributive extremal lattice are trim, thus left modular, that <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)\Longrightarrow(ii)\Longrightarrow(iii)\Longrightarrow(iv)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)\Longrightarrow(ii)\Longrightarrow(iii)\Longrightarrow(iv)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> is true for semidistributive lattices. By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma 2.17 ([]). ‣ 2.5. Congruence normal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.17</span></a>, these implications are true in particular for congruence uniform lattices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.7. </span>The order dimension of a poset</h3>

<div id="S2.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now recall what is the (order) dimension of a poset <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. The book <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> of W.T. Trotter is a good reference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.18</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem18.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{N}^{n},\leq)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{N}^{n},\leq)</annotation></semantics></math> be the poset defined by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})\leq(y_{1},\dots,y_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})\leq(y_{1},\dots,y_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\leq y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\leq y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dimension</span> of a poset <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is the smallest integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a subposet of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem18.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{N}^{n},\leq)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{N}^{n},\leq)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, this is the minimum number of linear extensions whose intersection is <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, where the intersection is the poset whose relations are <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\leq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq y</annotation></semantics></math> if in each of the chosen linear extensions we have <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\leq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\leq y</annotation></semantics></math>.
The posets of dimension smaller than <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> include, for example, the planar posets with a minimum <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, the interval orders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> and the posets whose Hasse diagrams contain at most one cycle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. It seems that for families of lattices the dimension is easier to determine. For example, a lattice is planar if and only if its dimension is smaller than <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. This is false for posets since there exist planar posets of any dimension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. Also, the following is a long-standing conjecture in dimension theory (see for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> for more details):</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 2.19</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> be two posets. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(P\times Q)\geq\dim(P)+\dim(Q)-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(P\times Q)\geq\dim(P)+\dim(Q)-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">But if <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> are lattices, we always have <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(P\times Q)=\dim(P)+\dim(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(P\times Q)=\dim(P)+\dim(Q)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a result of K. Baker that can be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. For some families of semidistributive extremal lattices, we will be able to find their dimension in <a href="#S4" title="4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We finish by recalling a general bound on the dimension of a poset <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S2.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">width</span> of a poset <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{width}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>width</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{width}(P)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the biggest size of an antichain of <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote by <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Dis}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Dis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Dis}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> the subposet of <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> on its dissectors, where a <span id="S2.SS7.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dissector</span> is an element <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\setminus[x,\hat{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\setminus[x,\hat{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> has a maximum element.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.20</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, Theorem 6]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem20.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any poset <math id="S2.Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{Dis}(P))\leq\dim(P)\leq\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(L))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>width</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Dis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>width</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{Dis}(P))\leq\dim(P)\leq\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(L)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">N. Reading gives two proofs of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem20" title="Proposition 2.20 ([, Theorem 6]). ‣ 2.7. The order dimension of a poset ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.20</span></a>. One of these proofs use results that we will recall in <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.
This implies the following result, which is proved similarly in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, Lemma 4.22]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.21</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a semidistributive lattice such that the maximum number of elements that cover an element of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(L)\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(L)\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in L</annotation></semantics></math> be an element covered by <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> elements. Let <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}=[x,\hat{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}=[x,\hat{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a semidistributive lattice. Any atom of <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dissector. Indeed, let <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> be an atom of <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{L^{\prime}}(x)\setminus F_{L^{\prime}}(y)=L^{\prime}\setminus[y,\hat{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{L^{\prime}}(x)\setminus F_{L^{\prime}}(y)=L^{\prime}\setminus[y,\hat{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is semidistributive and <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\lessdot y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\lessdot y</annotation></semantics></math> is a cover relation, it follows from <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Lemma 2.7 ([, Section 5]). ‣ 2.3. Semidistributive lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> that <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{L^{\prime}}(x)\setminus F_{L^{\prime}}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{L^{\prime}}(x)\setminus F_{L^{\prime}}(y)</annotation></semantics></math> has a maximum element. Thus <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}\setminus[y,\hat{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}\setminus[y,\hat{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> has a maximum, which means that <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is a dissector. Since each of the <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> atoms of <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dissector, then <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(L)\geq\dim(L^{\prime})\geq\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{Dis}(L^{\prime}))\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>width</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Dis</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(L)\geq\dim(L^{\prime})\geq\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{Dis}(L^{\prime}))\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>, which finishes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.8. </span>Representation theory of algebras</h3>

<div id="S2.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We finish this background by recalling some vocabulary of the representation theory of associative algebras, which will be only used in <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>. A good reference is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and for gentle algebras we recommend <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite-dimensional <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra, where <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is an algebraically closed field. We denote by <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mod}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mod}A</annotation></semantics></math> the category of finite-dimensional right <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-modules. Subcategories are assumed to be full and closed under finite direct sums and summands. The <span id="S2.SS8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">torsions classes</span> are the subcategories of <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mod}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mod}A</annotation></semantics></math> closed by extensions and quotients. They form a complete lattice with meet given by intersection, denoted by <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tors}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tors</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tors}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, that was extensively studied <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A module is called <span id="S2.SS8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">indecomposable</span> if it is not isomorphic to a direct sum of two non-zero modules. A <span id="S2.SS8.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">brick</span> <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-module such that <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{End}(B)\cong\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>End</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝕂</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{End}(B)\cong\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Two bricks <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S2.SS8.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hom-orthogonal</span> if <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Hom}(B,B^{\prime})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Hom}(B,B^{\prime})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Hom}(B^{\prime},B)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Hom}(B^{\prime},B)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. A <span id="S2.SS8.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semibrick</span> is a set <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B_{i}\}_{i\in I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B_{i}\}_{i\in I}</annotation></semantics></math> of pairwise hom-orthogonal bricks. The algebra <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS8.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">representation finite</span> if it has a finite number of non-isomorphic indecomposable modules. A <span id="S2.SS8.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cycle</span> in the category <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mod}\,A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mod}\,A</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of indecomposable modules <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{0},M_{1},\dots,M_{r}=M_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{0},M_{1},\dots,M_{r}=M_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and non-isomorphisms <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:M_{i}\rightarrow M_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:M_{i}\rightarrow M_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq r-1</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is known that if <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is representation finite and has no cycles, then its indecomposable modules are the bricks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Moreover, in this case <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tors}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tors</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tors}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is finite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.SS8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">quiver</span> <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=(Q_{0},Q_{1},s,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=(Q_{0},Q_{1},s,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a directed graph whose vertices are <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and its edges, called arrows, are <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> are two maps from <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> that respectively specify what is the source and target of an arrow. We will assume that <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is finite and connected. A <span id="S2.SS8.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">path</span> of length <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite sequence of arrows <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\dots\gamma_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\dots\gamma_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t(\gamma_{i})=s(\gamma_{i+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t(\gamma_{i})=s(\gamma_{i+1})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[m-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[m-1]</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}Q</annotation></semantics></math> the path algebra of <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and by <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> its ideal generated by arrows. A zero relation in <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a path <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\dots\gamma_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\dots\gamma_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. A two sided ideal <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}Q</annotation></semantics></math> is an <span id="S2.SS8.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">admissible ideal</span> if there exists <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{Q}^{m}\subseteq I\subseteq R_{Q}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>⊆</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{Q}^{m}\subseteq I\subseteq R_{Q}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the sequel <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is always assumed to be an admissible ideal generated by zero relations. For background on gentle algebras see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.22</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An algebra <math id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathbb{K}Q/I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathbb{K}Q/I</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S2.Thmtheorem22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">gentle algebra</span> if all the following properties are satisfied:</p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I2.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S2.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ideal <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by zero relations of length two.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I2.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S2.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are at most two incoming and two outgoing arrows at every vertex of <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, and there is at most one arrow <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and one arrow <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\neq\alpha\beta\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\neq\alpha\beta\in I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\neq\gamma\alpha\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\neq\gamma\alpha\in I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S2.I2.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S2.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every arrow <math id="S2.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, there is at most one arrow <math id="S2.I2.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and one arrow <math id="S2.I2.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.I2.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\beta\not\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\beta\not\in I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\alpha\not\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\alpha\not\in I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The only examples of gentle algebras that we will see are the <span id="S2.SS8.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">gentle trees</span>, which are the gentle algebras whose underlying undirected graph is a tree (see <a href="#S4.F14.sf1" title="In Figure 14 ‣ 4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14(a)</span></a>).
The gentle trees are representation finite and their indecomposables are certain modules called string modules (see <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.SS8.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">representation</span> <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the following data: to each vertex <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in Q_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in Q_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> we associate a <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, and to each arrow <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:x\rightarrow y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:x\rightarrow y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we associate a <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear map <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{\alpha}:M_{x}\rightarrow M_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{\alpha}:M_{x}\rightarrow M_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a representation is denoted by <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M_{x},\varphi_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M_{x},\varphi_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> has zero relations, we ask additionally that the composition of maps corresponding to a zero relation gives the zero linear map. If <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M_{x},\varphi_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M_{x},\varphi_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M^{\prime}_{x},\varphi^{\prime}_{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M^{\prime}_{x},\varphi^{\prime}_{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> are two representations of <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, a morphism <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> between these two representations is a family <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=(f_{x})_{x\in Q_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=(f_{x})_{x\in Q_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear maps that satisfy <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi^{\prime}_{\alpha}f_{x}=f_{y}\varphi_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi^{\prime}_{\alpha}f_{x}=f_{y}\varphi_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:x\rightarrow y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:x\rightarrow y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The representations of <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> with their morphisms form an abelian category which is equivalent to the category <math id="S2.SS8.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mod}\,\mathbb{K}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mod}\,\mathbb{K}Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we will allow ourselves to switch between the two languages.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>The main results</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Edge-labellings and left modularity</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we give a result that characterizes left modularity as the equality of some edge-labellings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a lattice. Denote by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\,\hat{0}=x_{0}&lt;\dots&lt;x_{k}=\hat{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\,\hat{0}=x_{0}&lt;\dots&lt;x_{k}=\hat{1}</annotation></semantics></math> a chain containing <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, we have four labellings <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of the cover relations of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> (the edges of its Hasse diagram). For <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, denote <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(j):=\mathrm{min}\{i\,|\,j\leq x_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(j):=\mathrm{min}\{i\,|\,j\leq x_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>MIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, denote <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(m):=\mathrm{max}\{i\,|\,m\geq x_{i-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(m):=\mathrm{max}\{i\,|\,m\geq x_{i-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for a cover relation <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math> we define</p>
