<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following was partly stated as <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> in the introduction:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Using the notations from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, for any lattice <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}\leq\gamma_{1}=\gamma_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}\leq\gamma_{1}=\gamma_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}^{\prime}=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}^{\prime}=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Before giving the proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, we give two lemmas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Using the same notations from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}=\gamma_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}=\gamma_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math> be a cover relation.
We only prove <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as the argument for <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}=\gamma_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}=\gamma_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is dual.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Denote <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}:=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}(b\lessdot c)=\mathrm{min}\{i\,|\,b\vee x_{i}\geq c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}:=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}(b\lessdot c)=\mathrm{min}\{i\,|\,b\vee x_{i}\geq c\}</annotation></semantics></math>. It means that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee x_{i_{0}}\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee x_{i_{0}}\geq c</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee x_{i_{0}-1}\not\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≱</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee x_{i_{0}-1}\not\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us prove that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}=\gamma_{2}(b\lessdot c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}=\gamma_{2}(b\lessdot c)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, let us prove that <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}(b\lessdot c)\leq i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}(b\lessdot c)\leq i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We just have to prove that for any <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> meet-irreducible such that <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\not\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≱</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\not\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(m)\leq i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(m)\leq i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Seeking a contradiction, suppose <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(m)&gt;i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(m)&gt;i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by definition of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq x_{i_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq x_{i_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> since the <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s form a chain. Since <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq x_{i_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq x_{i_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq b\vee x_{i_{0}}\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq b\vee x_{i_{0}}\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>. It is absurd because <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\not\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≱</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\not\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>, which proves that <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}(b\lessdot c)\leq i_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}(b\lessdot c)\leq i_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, let us prove that <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}\leq\gamma_{2}(b\lessdot c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}\leq\gamma_{2}(b\lessdot c)</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we want to find a meet-irreducible <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq b</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\not\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≱</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\not\geq c</annotation></semantics></math> and such that <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}\leq\beta(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}\leq\beta(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. The latter condition is equivalent to <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq x_{i_{0}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq x_{i_{0}-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. So we want to find a meet-irreducible <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq b\vee x_{i_{0}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq b\vee x_{i_{0}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\not\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≱</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\not\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a meet-irreducible exists because <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee x_{i_{0}-1}\not\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≱</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee x_{i_{0}-1}\not\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>, as it was noted in the beginning. If such a meet-irreducible does not exist, then all the meet-irreducibles above <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee x_{i_{0}-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee x_{i_{0}-1}</annotation></semantics></math> would also be above <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, and since an element is the meet of the meet-irreducibles above it, then <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee x_{i_{0}-1}\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee x_{i_{0}-1}\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>, which is absurd. Thus <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{0}\leq\gamma_{2}(b\lessdot c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{0}\leq\gamma_{2}(b\lessdot c)</annotation></semantics></math>. This finishes the proof of <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Using the same notations as in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}^{\prime}\leq\gamma_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}^{\prime}\leq\gamma_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math> be a cover relation. Denote <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i:=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}(b\lessdot c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i:=\gamma_{2}^{\prime}(b\lessdot c)</annotation></semantics></math>. It means that <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee x_{i}\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee x_{i}\geq c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee x_{i-1}\not\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≱</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee x_{i-1}\not\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>. We want to prove that <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}^{\prime}(b\lessdot c)\geq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}^{\prime}(b\lessdot c)\geq i</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equivalent to <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\wedge x_{i-1}\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\wedge x_{i-1}\leq b</annotation></semantics></math>. Seeking a contradiction, suppose that <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\wedge x_{i-1}\not\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≰</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\wedge x_{i-1}\not\leq b</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&lt;c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&lt;c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\wedge x_{i-1}\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\wedge x_{i-1}\leq c</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\leq b\vee(c\wedge x_{i-1})\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\leq b\vee(c\wedge x_{i-1})\leq c</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\wedge x_{i-1}\not\leq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≰</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\wedge x_{i-1}\not\leq b</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee(c\wedge x_{i-1})\neq b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee(c\wedge x_{i-1})\neq b</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee(c\wedge x_{i-1})=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee(c\wedge x_{i-1})=c</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee x_{i-1}\geq b\vee(c\wedge x_{i-1})=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee x_{i-1}\geq b\vee(c\wedge x_{i-1})=c</annotation></semantics></math>, which is absurd.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text"> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> give the first part of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. For the rest, using <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx4.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\gamma_{1}^{\prime}\neq\gamma_{2}^{\prime}\iff\exists i,\,\exists\,b\lessdot c,\,\,\left\{\begin{array}[]{cc}\gamma_{2}^{\prime}(b\lessdot c)\leq i\\
\gamma_{1}^{\prime}(b\lessdot c)\geq i+1.\end{array}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">∃</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gamma_{1}^{\prime}\neq\gamma_{2}^{\prime}\iff\exists i,\,\exists\,b\lessdot c,\,\,\left\{\begin{array}[]{cc}\gamma_{2}^{\prime}(b\lessdot c)\leq i\\
\gamma_{1}^{\prime}(b\lessdot c)\geq i+1.\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">By definition of <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is also equivalent to the fact that there exist an <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and a cover <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following subposet of <math id="S3.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a sublattice:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS1.p11.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="134.94" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 84.75 134.94" width="84.75"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,134.94) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(39.11,0) translate(0,19.26)"><g stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 23.62 47.24 L 0 94.49 L -23.62 66.14 L -23.62 28.35 L 0 0"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -23.62 66.14 L -23.62 28.35"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -16.94 -12.58)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.45em;--ltx-fo-height:0.56em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.76" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.69)" width="33.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p11.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\wedge x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\wedge x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 23.62 47.24"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 28.51 45.3)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.03" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="12.52"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p11.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 94.49"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -16.91 101.45)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="33.83"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p11.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\vee x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\vee x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -23.62 66.14"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -34.5 63.16)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="5.96" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="5.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p11.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -23.62 28.35"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -34.45 23.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="5.94"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS1.p11.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_align_center">
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem13" title="Proposition 2.13 ([]). ‣ 2.4. Left modular lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a>, we deduce that <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}^{\prime}\neq\gamma_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}^{\prime}\neq\gamma_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the fact that there exists <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is not left modular. This finishes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">S.-C. Liu proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, for the case of a longest chain <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}^{\prime}\leq\gamma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}^{\prime}\leq\gamma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and that if for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{2}^{\prime}=\gamma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{2}^{\prime}=\gamma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. He also proved that the equal labellings that we obtain starting from a maximal left modular chain are <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EL</annotation></semantics></math>-labellings. What we add to the story, other than an easier proof (by considering <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> we also add some symmetry), is a way to use these labellings to prove that lattices are left modular.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">P. McNamara and H. Thomas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> proved that the above equal labellings of a left modular lattice are interpolating labellings (an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EL</annotation></semantics></math>-labelling with an additional property), and also proved that if a finite lattice has an interpolating labelling, then it is left modular. Our Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> is then of the same flavor, helping us prove left modularity, but without mentioning <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EL</annotation></semantics></math>-labelling.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the definition of the bijection <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S2" title="2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We can use <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> to give a short proof of the following result:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Semidistributive extremal lattices are left modular.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is extremal, the choice of any longest chain <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\,\hat{0}=x_{0}\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{n}=\hat{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⋖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\,\hat{0}=x_{0}\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{n}=\hat{1}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a numbering of the join and meet-irreducibles <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{1},j_{2},\dots,j_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{1},j_{2},\dots,j_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{1},m_{2},\dots,m_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S3.SS1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}=j_{1}\vee\dots\vee j_{i}=m_{i+1}\wedge\dots\wedge m_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}=j_{1}\vee\dots\vee j_{i}=m_{i+1}\wedge\dots\wedge m_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(j_{i})=m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(j_{i})=m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Proposition 2.9. ‣ 2.3. Semidistributive lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>. Moreover <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(j_{i})=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(j_{i})=i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta(m_{i})=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta(m_{i})=i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is semidistributive, using <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 2.8 ([] and [, Proposition 6.2]). ‣ 2.3. Semidistributive lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> we have that <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{J}(b\lessdot c)=j_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{J}(b\lessdot c)=j_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{M}(b\lessdot c)=m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{M}(b\lessdot c)=m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus with <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Remark 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Remark</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we have <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{1}=\gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{1}=\gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> gives that <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> was proved and first used in order to obtain the left modularity of the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(P,\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(P,\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>-Tamari lattices (defined by the author in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we characterize the congruence normal lattices that are extremal or left modular by necessary and sufficient conditions on each doubling step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the sequel, unless stated otherwise, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a convex subset of a lattice <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We call the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf heart}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐡𝐞𝐚𝐫𝐭</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf heart}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, written <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C)</annotation></semantics></math>, all the elements of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> that are less than all the maximal elements of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and bigger than all its minimal elements.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any convex subset <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if and only if the meet of the maximal elements is bigger than the join of the minimal elements of </span><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. In this case, if these elements are respectively given by </span><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in I</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in J</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, we have </span><math id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(C)=[\vee_{i\in I}m_{i}\,,\,\wedge_{j\in J}M_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∨</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∧</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C)=[\vee_{i\in I}m_{i}\,,\,\wedge_{j\in J}M_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is convex.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C)=C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C)=C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if and only if </span><math id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an interval.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I1.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iv)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iv)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(C)=\{a\in C\,|\,\forall b\in C,\,a\vee b\in C\,\,\text{and}\,\,a\wedge b\in C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C)=\{a\in C\,|\,\forall b\in C,\,a\vee b\in C\,\,\text{and}\,\,a\wedge b\in C\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The statement <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> is easy to prove and implies <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us prove <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iv)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iv)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in C</annotation></semantics></math>. There exists a minimal element <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in C</annotation></semantics></math> and a maximal element <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\in C</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq b\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq b\leq M</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H(C)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq a\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq a\leq M</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq a\vee b\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq a\vee b\leq M</annotation></semantics></math>, thus by convexity of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\vee b\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\vee b\in C</annotation></semantics></math>. Also <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq a\wedge b\leq a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq a\wedge b\leq a</annotation></semantics></math> implies by convexity that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\wedge b\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\wedge b\in C</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a\in\{a\in C\,|\,\forall b\in C,\,a\vee b\in C\,\,\text{and}\,\,a\wedge b\in C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mtext>and</mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\{a\in C\,|\,\forall b\in C,\,a\vee b\in C\,\,\text{and}\,\,a\wedge b\in C\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in C</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\vee b\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\vee b\in C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\wedge b\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\wedge b\in C</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a maximal element and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> a minimal element of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. We have by hypothesis that <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\vee M\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\vee M\in C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\wedge m\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\wedge m\in C</annotation></semantics></math>. But since <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is maximal and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, this happens if and only if <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\leq M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\geq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\geq m</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is smaller than all the maximal elements of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and bigger than all the minimal elements of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The next two results were partly stated as <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> in the introduction:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> be a congruence normal lattice. Then</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I2.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is join-extremal if and only if it is join-congruence uniform and </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> intersects the spine of </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for all </span><math id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I2.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have the dual result for meet-extremality by replacing </span><em id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph">lower</em><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> by </span><em id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph">upper</em><span id="S3.I2.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I2.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is extremal if and only if it is congruence uniform and </span><math id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> intersects the spine of </span><math id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for all </span><math id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is congruence normal we construct it by successive doublings of convex subsets starting from the one element lattice, which is extremal. By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem15" title="Lemma 2.15 ([, Lemma 1]). ‣ 2.5. Congruence normal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.15</span></a>, at each doubling of the convex subset <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we add exactly as many join-irreducibles as the number of minimal elements of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we add as many meet-irreducibles as the number of maximal elements of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 2.16. ‣ 2.5. Congruence normal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.16</span></a> the doubling of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> will add <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to the length of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain an element of the spine and <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. Thus, we keep the initial join-extremality through the doubling procedure if and only if at each step <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a lower-pseudo interval that contains an element of the spine. Indeed, these are the subsets that have a minimum element. Similarly for meet-extremality with upper pseudo-intervals. Finally, for extremality we need <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to have a minimum and a maximum, and since it is convex this means that it has to be an interval. This finishes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> be a congruence normal lattice. Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> has an element that lies on a maximal left modular chain of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To prove <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 3.12. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.12</span></a>, we will need some lemmas. The next two results follow from <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem14" title="Definition 2.14. ‣ 2.5. Congruence normal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.14</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,\epsilon)\in L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,\epsilon)\in L[C]</annotation></semantics></math>. We have</span></p>
