<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">We want to conclude that <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,\epsilon)\vee(b,\alpha)=(a,\epsilon)\vee(c,\alpha^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,\epsilon)\vee(b,\alpha)=(a,\epsilon)\vee(c,\alpha^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,\epsilon)\vee(b,\alpha)=(a,\epsilon)\vee(c,\alpha^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,\epsilon)\vee(b,\alpha)=(a,\epsilon)\vee(c,\alpha^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, so it suffices to show that one of the conditions on the RHS of (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Proof. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is false.
Suppose that <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem14" title="Lemma 3.14. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a>, since <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\neq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\neq c</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in I_{L}(C)\setminus C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in I_{L}(C)\setminus C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\not\in I_{L}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\not\in I_{L}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\vee c\not\in I_{L}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\vee c\not\in I_{L}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\wedge b\not\in\big(L\setminus I_{L}(C)\big)\cup C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\wedge b\not\in\big(L\setminus I_{L}(C)\big)\cup C</annotation></semantics></math>, which concludes.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">It follows from <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem13" title="Proposition 2.13 ([]). ‣ 2.4. Left modular lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a> that <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b,\alpha)\lessdot(c,\alpha^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⋖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b,\alpha)\lessdot(c,\alpha^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\neq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\neq c</annotation></semantics></math> is a counterexample to the left modularity of <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, assume that <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in L</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(b,\alpha)\lessdot(c,\alpha^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⋖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b,\alpha)\lessdot(c,\alpha^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be a cover in <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\neq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\neq c</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\neq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\neq c</annotation></semantics></math>, we have by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem14" title="Lemma 3.14. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.14</span></a> that <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\lessdot c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\lessdot c</annotation></semantics></math> is a cover in <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular it implies by <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem13" title="Proposition 2.13 ([]). ‣ 2.4. Left modular lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a> that <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\vee b\neq a\vee c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\vee b\neq a\vee c</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\wedge b\neq a\wedge c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\wedge b\neq a\wedge c</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition of the join in <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math>, it is clear that <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,\epsilon)\vee(b,\alpha)\neq(a,\epsilon)\vee(c,\alpha^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,\epsilon)\vee(b,\alpha)\neq(a,\epsilon)\vee(c,\alpha^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,\epsilon)\wedge(b,\alpha)\neq(a,\epsilon)\wedge(c,\alpha^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,\epsilon)\wedge(b,\alpha)\neq(a,\epsilon)\wedge(c,\alpha^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, which again using <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem13" title="Proposition 2.13 ([]). ‣ 2.4. Left modular lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a> finishes the proof.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
