"--ltx-fo-width:0.79em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.88"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS2.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;m</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&lt;m&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&lt;m&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, which is absurd since <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\lessdot b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\lessdot b</annotation></semantics></math> is a cover relation. Thus <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\not&lt;m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≮</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\not&lt;m</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math>, then by convexity of <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;a&lt;M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;a&lt;M</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in C</annotation></semantics></math>, which is absurd. Thus <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\not&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≮</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\not&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> are incomparable. Then <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\vee m=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\vee m=b</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is bigger than <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, it implies <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\geq a\vee m=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\geq a\vee m=b</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;b&lt;M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;b&lt;M</annotation></semantics></math>, which by convexity of <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in C</annotation></semantics></math>. This is absurd, which proves that <math id="S3.SS2.p21.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.18</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a lattice and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> a convex subset that does not contain an element of any maximal left modular chain. Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> is not left modular.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">We need to prove that <math id="S3.SS2.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> does not have a maximal left modular chain.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">To begin with, no such chain goes through the doubling, meaning containing an edge <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,0)\lessdot(c,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⋖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,0)\lessdot(c,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in C</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, if it was the case, take the projection of this chain to <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning replacing the elements <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> of this chain by <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. By forgetting the repetition of <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> coming from <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,0)\lessdot(c,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⋖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,0)\lessdot(c,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem15" title="Lemma 3.15. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.15</span></a> this would give a maximal left modular chain of <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. It is absurd because <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> would contain the element <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> of this chain but <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain any element of a maximal left modular chain of <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> by hypothesis.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to prove that we do not have a maximal left modular chain of <math id="S3.SS2.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> that does not go through the doubling. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma 3.17. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.17</span></a> for <math id="S3.SS2.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\times\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\times\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, if such a chain exists we would have an element <math id="S3.SS2.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> of this chain that is neither smaller than all the maximal elements of <math id="S3.SS2.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\times\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\times\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math> nor bigger than all its minimal elements. It means that <math id="S3.SS2.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is neither smaller than all the maximal elements of <math id="S3.SS2.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> nor bigger than all its minimal elements. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem16" title="Proposition 3.16. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a> the element <math id="S3.SS2.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> would not be left modular in <math id="S3.SS2.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then such a maximal left modular chain does not exist.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Now we know that if <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular it is necessary that there exists a maximal left modular chain <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> that goes through the doubling, meaning it comes from a left modular chain <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> that intersects <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem16" title="Proposition 3.16. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a> this intersection contains an element of <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. The following lemma proves that this is also a sufficient condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.19</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a lattice and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> a convex subset of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that there exists <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H(C)</annotation></semantics></math> that lies on a maximal left modular chain <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta:\,x_{0}\lessdot x_{1}\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{l}:=a\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⋖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta:\,x_{0}\lessdot x_{1}\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{l}:=a\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the maximal chain</span></p>
