<p id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximal left modular chain. Thus <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in L</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular and <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H(C)</annotation></semantics></math>, we know using <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem16" title="Proposition 3.16. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a> that <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,0)\lessdot(a,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⋖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,0)\lessdot(a,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a cover relation made of two left modular elements of <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math>. Since (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Lemma 3.19. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is a maximal chain, it just remains to prove that any of its elements other than <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,1)</annotation></semantics></math> are left modular in <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem16" title="Proposition 3.16. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a> since for any other element <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> of the chain (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Lemma 3.19. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) we have <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in L</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular and either smaller or bigger than <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p27.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 3.12. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.12</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.9" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 3.12. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.12</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem19" title="Lemma 3.19. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a> we know that if for all <math id="S3.SS2.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> we have that <math id="S3.SS2.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> has an element that lies on a maximal left modular chain of <math id="S3.SS2.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS2.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose that <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. We want to prove that <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> has an element that lies on a maximal left modular chain of <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem18" title="Lemma 3.18. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.18</span></a>, it is required that <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains an element of a maximal left modular chain of <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, if at some point no such chain exists in our lattice then it is too late; we will never recover a left modular lattice by doublings.
As recalled at the end of <a href="#S2" title="2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, all maximal left modular chains are of longest length. With the previous requirement, we know that at each doubling step we add one to the length of the lattice. Then to have a maximal left modular chain after the doubling, since it is a longest chain, we need it to go through the doubling. Thus by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem16" title="Proposition 3.16. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.16</span></a> it is forced that <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> contains an element that lies on a maximal left modular chain of <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p30.1" class="ltx_p">Before giving some corollaries of our results, we give a lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.20</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> be an extremal congruence uniform lattice. Then all the elements of the spine of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> are left modular elements.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.10" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove this by induction on the number of doublings. For the one element lattice, the spine is the lattice itself and the only element is left modular. By induction, assume that all the elements of the spine of <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> are left modular. We want to prove that all the elements of the spine of <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}]=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}][C_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}]=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}][C_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> are left modular.
We have the equivalence between the fact that all the elements of the spine are left modular and the fact that any longest chain is a maximal left modular chain. Thus the induction hypothesis gives us that the longest chains of <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> are maximal left modular chains.
By <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Proposition 3.11. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>, we have that <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects the spine of <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 2.16. ‣ 2.5. Congruence normal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.16</span></a> that the longest chains of <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> go through the doubling; these correspond to the longest chains of <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> intersecting <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Precisely, these are the chains (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Lemma 3.19. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem19" title="Lemma 3.19. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a> for a longest chain <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta:x_{0}\lessdot x_{1}\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{l}:=a\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⋖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>⋖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⋖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta:x_{0}\lessdot x_{1}\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{l}:=a\lessdot\dots\lessdot x_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is an interval, by <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 3.10. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a> we have <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C_{i})=C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C_{i})=C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H(C_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H(C_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S3.SS2.p31.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximal left modular chain by hypothesis, by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem19" title="Lemma 3.19. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a> the longest chains (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Lemma 3.19. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) are also maximal left modular chains, which finishes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.21</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> is congruence uniform, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains an element of the spine of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.11" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <math id="S3.SS2.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 3.10. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.10</span></a>, we have <math id="S3.SS2.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(C_{i})=C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(C_{i})=C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.SS2.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 3.12. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.12</span></a> tells us that <math id="S3.SS2.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is left modular if and only if <math id="S3.SS2.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has an element that lies on a maximal left modular chain of <math id="S3.SS2.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.SS2.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Then using the fact that the maximal left modular chains are of longest length gives one direction of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Corollary 3.21. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>, the other direction is obtained using additionally <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem20" title="Lemma 3.20. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.20</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.22</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Join-congruence uniform left modular lattices are join-extremal. Similarly meet-congruence uniform left modular lattices are meet-extremal.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For congruence uniform lattices, extremality and left modularity are equivalent.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.12" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first statements on join and meet-congruence uniform lattices are a direct consequence of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem12" title="Theorem 3.12. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.12</span></a> with the fact that maximal left modular chains are of longest length. The statement on congruence uniform lattices is the comparison of <math id="S3.SS2.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2)</annotation></semantics></math> of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Proposition 3.11. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a> and Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Corollary 3.21. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.23</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.Thmtheorem20" title="Lemma 3.20. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.20</span></a> is a special case of the combination of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, Lemma 6]</cite> and of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite>, but is proved in our context by a simple induction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The first statements of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem22" title="Corollary 3.22. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.22</span></a> relative to join and meet-congruence uniform lattices are a special case of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, Theorem 3.2]</cite>, as these lattices are respectively join and meet-semidistributive as recalled in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem17" title="Lemma 2.17 ([]). ‣ 2.5. Congruence normal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.17</span></a>. Combining this result with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Theorem 1.4]</cite> gives, as was observed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, a generalization of the last statement of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem22" title="Corollary 3.22. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.22</span></a> to all semidistributive lattices.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Shellability of congruence normal lattices</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the definitions related to shellability from <a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6. Shellability ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>.
In the sequel <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is always a non-empty convex subset of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We give a necessary condition for a congruence normal lattice to be shellable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.24</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> be a congruence normal lattice. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is shellable, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects the spine of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will prove <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 3.24. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.24</span></a> in the rest of this section, but we start with two corollaries:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.25</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If a join-congruence uniform lattice is shellable, then it is join-extremal. If a meet-congruence uniform lattice is shellable, then it is meet-extremal.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem11" title="Proposition 3.11. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Propositions</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 3.24. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.24</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following was stated as <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> in the introduction, and contains <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.26</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem26.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a congruence uniform lattice. The following properties are equivalent:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I3.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is extremal.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I3.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is left modular.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I3.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I3.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="S3.I3.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EL" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EL</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-shellable.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S3.I3.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iv)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iv)</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S3.I3.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is shellable.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.Thmtheorem22" title="Corollary 3.22. ‣ 3.2. Extremality and left modularity of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.22</span></a><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text"> gives <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)\Longleftrightarrow(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)\Longleftrightarrow(ii)</annotation></semantics></math>. As we recalled in <a href="#S2" title="2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we have that <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)\Longrightarrow(iii)\Longrightarrow(iv)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)\Longrightarrow(iii)\Longrightarrow(iv)</annotation></semantics></math> is true for any lattice. It remains to prove <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iv)\Longrightarrow(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iv)\Longrightarrow(i)</annotation></semantics></math>, which follows from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem25" title="Corollary 3.25. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.25</span></a> since a congruence uniform lattice is both join-congruence uniform and meet-congruence uniform.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We are now interested in proving <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 3.24. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.24</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.27</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a chain of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cap C=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cap C=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a chain in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> that we denote <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cap C:=\{c_{1},c_{2},\dots,c_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cap C:=\{c_{1},c_{2},\dots,c_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and we denote by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)</annotation></semantics></math> the chain</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(F\setminus(F\,\cap C)\big)\,\cup\,\{(c_{1},0),(c_{2},0),\dots,(c_{i},0)\}\,\cup\,\{(c_{i},1),(c_{i+1},1),\dots,(c_{k},1)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.392em">∩</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.392em">∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.392em">∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(F\setminus(F\,\cap C)\big)\,\cup\,\{(c_{1},0),(c_{2},0),\dots,(c_{i},0)\}\,\cup\,\{(c_{i},1),(c_{i+1},1),\dots,(c_{k},1)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Although it is in conflict with our convention that for <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]=\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]=\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, it turns out to be convenient to define <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0]=\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0]=\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma follows from the definition of the doubling construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.28</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem28.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The facets of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(L[C])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(L[C])</annotation></semantics></math> are all the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)</annotation></semantics></math> for any maximal chain <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[|F\cap C|]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[|F\cap C|]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For any finite lattice <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a directed graph <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FA(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FA(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <span id="S3.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">facet adjacency graph</span> of <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, whose vertices are the maximal chains of <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, and we have an edge <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\rightarrow G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\rightarrow G</annotation></semantics></math> between two such chains <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|F\,\cap G|=|G|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.392em">∩</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|F\,\cap G|=|G|-1</annotation></semantics></math>. A <span id="S3.SS3.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">source set</span> of a graph <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-empty subset of the vertices <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subsetneq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊊</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subsetneq V</annotation></semantics></math> such that there are no <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\rightarrow x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\rightarrow x</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\not\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\not\in X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that no edges starting outside <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> are pointing to a vertex of <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Two source sets <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> are disjoint if <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\,\cap Y=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.392em">∩</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\,\cap Y=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. See <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for an example of a non-extremal congruence uniform lattice, and <a href="#S3.F4" title="In 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for the case of an extremal congruence uniform lattice. In these figures we write the chains as words without including <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mn>0</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mn>1</mn><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
