<div id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since for any <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)\in S</annotation></semantics></math> there exists <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in C</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(c,0),(c,1)\}\subseteq F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(c,0),(c,1)\}\subseteq F</annotation></semantics></math>, and no maximal chain of <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> outside of <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> contains such elements, we know that no edges from a vertex outside <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is pointing to <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FA(L[C])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FA(L[C])</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves that <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a source set of <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FA(L[C])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FA(L[C])</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Seeking a contradiction, suppose that there exist <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)\not\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)\not\in T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,0)\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,0)\in T</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)\rightarrow(G,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)\rightarrow(G,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)\not\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)\not\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cap C\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cap C\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|(G,0)|=|G|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|(G,0)|=|G|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|(F,i)|=|F|+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|(F,i)|=|F|+1</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. In general, if <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\rightarrow F^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\rightarrow F^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FA(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FA(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|F|\geq|F^{\prime}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|F|\geq|F^{\prime}|</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)\rightarrow(G,0)\Longrightarrow|F|+1\geq|G|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)\rightarrow(G,0)\Longrightarrow|F|+1\geq|G|</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> does not intersect the spine of <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem16" title="Lemma 2.16. ‣ 2.5. Congruence normal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.16</span></a> we have <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(L[C])=\ell(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(L[C])=\ell(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|G|\leq|F|+1=|(F,i)|\leq\ell(L[C])=\ell(L)=|G|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|G|\leq|F|+1=|(F,i)|\leq\ell(L[C])=\ell(L)=|G|</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|(F,i)|=|G|=\ell(L[C])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|(F,i)|=|G|=\ell(L[C])</annotation></semantics></math>, thus <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)</annotation></semantics></math> is a longest chain of <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)\not\in T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)\not\in T</annotation></semantics></math>, which means <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is not a longest chain of <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, it implies that <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)\in S</annotation></semantics></math> since the chains of longest length of <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L[C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L[C]</annotation></semantics></math> are either longest chains of <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> or go through the doubling of <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, as observed in the first part of the proof, <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)</annotation></semantics></math> has at least two elements <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,1)</annotation></semantics></math> not in <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, which with <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|(F,i)|=|(G,0)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|(F,i)|=|(G,0)|</annotation></semantics></math> makes it absurd to have <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|(F,i)\,\cap(G,0)|=|(G,0)|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|(F,i)\,\cap(G,0)|=|(G,0)|-1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is implied by <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F,i)\rightarrow(G,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F,i)\rightarrow(G,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows that <math id="S3.SS3.p18.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a source set.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">With <math id="S3.SS3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\,\cap T=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.392em">∩</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\,\cap T=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.SS3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> does not intersect the spine of <math id="S3.SS3.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, this finishes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Now we can prove <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 3.24. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.24</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem24" title="Theorem 3.24. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.24</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> be a congruence normal lattice. Suppose that there exists <math id="S3.SS3.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS3.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> does not intersect the spine of <math id="S3.SS3.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i-1}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem33" title="Lemma 3.33. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.33</span></a> the graph <math id="S3.SS3.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FA(E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FA(E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}])</annotation></semantics></math> has at least two disjoint source sets. By <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem32" title="Lemma 3.32. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.32</span></a>, since <math id="S3.SS3.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=E[C_{1},\dots,C_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="S3.SS3.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[C_{1},\dots,C_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> by doublings, we have that <math id="S3.SS3.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> has also at least two disjoint source sets, which by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem30" title="Lemma 3.30. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.30</span></a> implies that <math id="S3.SS3.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is not shellable.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we give, using the fundamental theorem of finite semidistributive lattices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>, a counterexample to the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem34" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 3.34</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Question 2.5.5]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem34.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem34.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem34.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem34.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Is an induced subcomplex of a canonical join complex still a canonical join complex?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As explained in <a href="#S2" title="2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, for us a canonical join complex is always one coming from a semidistributive lattice (not just a join-semidistributive lattice). <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem34" title="Question 3.34 ([, Question 2.5.5]). ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Question</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.34</span></a> was asked in this context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The canonical join complex being the clique complex of its 1-skeleton, which is the canonical join graph, this question is equivalent to asking whether an induced subgraph of a join canonical graph is still such a graph. Indeed, an induced subcomplex on vertices <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> of a flag simplicial complex <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is still flag, and its <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-skeleton is the induced subgraph of the <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-skeleton of <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> on vertices <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
