<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a multigraph, we say that it admits a <em id="S3.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">two-acyclic factorization system</em> if there exists an orientation of its edges such that the resulting directed multigraph <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,\rightarrow)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,\rightarrow)</annotation></semantics></math> forms a two-acyclic factorization system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The following is a reformulation of results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem37" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.37</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem37.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem37.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem37.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem37.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The canonical join graphs are the complements of the multigraphs that admit a two-acyclic factorization system.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, Theorem 1.2]</cite>, a multigraph <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> admits a two-acyclic factorization system if and only if <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can be oriented such that the resulting directed multigraph <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,\rightarrow)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,\rightarrow)</annotation></semantics></math> gives a semidistributive lattice by taking the associated lattice of maximal orthogonal pairs. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, Theorem 5.13]</cite>, this is equivalent to <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G}</annotation></semantics></math> being a canonical join graph. This concludes.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S3.F5" title="In 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we give an example of the Galois graph <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> of a semidistributive extremal lattice. Indeed, conditions <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem36" title="Definition 3.36 ([]). ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.36</span></a> are satisfied since there are no cycles, and for condition <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math> we have only one regular arrow <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}\rightarrow v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}\rightarrow v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, this condition is satisfied since <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}\mathrel{\hbox to11.58pt{\vbox to4.28pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 0.09999pt\lower-2.13869pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke=#000000} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill=#000000} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.4pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {
