</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For a graph <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[X]</annotation></semantics></math> the induced subgraph of <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on the elements of <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X:=V(H)\setminus\{t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:=V(H)\setminus\{t\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is the vertex-set of <math id="S3.SS4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our goal is now to prove that <math id="S3.SS4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H[X]</annotation></semantics></math> is not a canonical join graph. Thus it will be a counterexample to <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem34" title="Question 3.34 ([, Question 2.5.5]). ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Question</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.34</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is easy:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem38" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem38.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.38</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem38.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem38.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem38.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem38.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem38.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a multigraph and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem38.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math>. We have
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem38.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G[X]}=\overline{G}[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G[X]}=\overline{G}[X]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">By definition, <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H}=G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H}=G(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem38" title="Lemma 3.38. ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.38</span></a>, we have <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{H[X]}=\overline{H}[X]=G(L)[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{H[X]}=\overline{H}[X]=G(L)[X]</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem37" title="Proposition 3.37. ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.37</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H[X]</annotation></semantics></math> is a canonical join graph if and only if the underlying undirected graph of <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)[X]</annotation></semantics></math>, or any multigraph obtained from it by adding some edges between two elements of <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)[X]</annotation></semantics></math> already joined by an edge, is a two-acyclic factorization system. The underlying undirected graph of <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)[X]</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <em id="S3.SS4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Grötzsch graph</em>; it is a triangle-free graph of chromatic number <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. These two properties will play an important role.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem39" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem39.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.39</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem39.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem39.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem39.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem39.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem39.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a simple graph. There exists an orientation of the edges of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem39.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that the resulting directed graph has no directed walk of length <math id="S3.Thmtheorem39.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem39.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(G)\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(G)\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(G)\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(G)\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us choose a proper coloring <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:V\rightarrow\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:V\rightarrow\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We deduce an orientation of the edges of <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by orienting an edge <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\rightarrow y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\rightarrow y</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(x)&lt;C(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(x)&lt;C(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is an orientation of <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> without directed walks of length <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, suppose that there exists an orientation of the edges of <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that the resulting directed graph <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,\rightarrow)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,\rightarrow)</annotation></semantics></math> has no directed walk of length <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,\rightarrow)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,\rightarrow)</annotation></semantics></math> does not have cycles. This proves that the following coloring is well-defined; color any vertex <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with the longest length of a directed walk ending at <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives a coloring <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:V\rightarrow\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:V\rightarrow\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is proper since for each <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\rightarrow y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\rightarrow y</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(x)&lt;C(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(x)&lt;C(y)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem40" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem40.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.40</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem40.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem40.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem40.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem40.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A triangle-free simple directed multigraph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem40.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> forms a two-acyclic factorization system if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem40.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> does not have directed walk of length <math id="S3.Thmtheorem40.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is simple, conditions <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(ii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(ii)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem36" title="Definition 3.36 ([]). ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.36</span></a> are satisfied. To the contrary, condition <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be satisfied if there are any <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> arrows since <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is triangle-free. Thus <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> forms a two-acyclic factorization system if and only if <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> does not have regular arrows <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>. By definitions of <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrel{\hbox to11.58pt{\vbox to4.28pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 0.09999pt\lower-2.13869pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke=#000000} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill=#000000} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.4pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {
