<div id="S3.SS4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p16.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is a consequence of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem39" title="Lemma 3.39. ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemmas</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.39</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem40" title="Lemma 3.40. ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.40</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem41" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem41.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.41</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem41.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem41.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem41.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem41.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A triangle-free simple graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem41.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> admits a two-acyclic factorization system if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem41.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(G)\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(G)\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem42" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem42.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.42</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem42.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem42.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem42.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem42.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem42.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a triangle-free multigraph. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem42.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be its underlying simple graph, which is obtained by replacing multiple edges by a single edge. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem42.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> admits a two-acyclic factorization system, then it is also the case of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem42.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,\rightarrow)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,\rightarrow)</annotation></semantics></math> be an orientation of <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that forms a two-acyclic factorization system. From it we deduce an orientation <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(G^{\prime},\rightarrow^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G^{\prime},\rightarrow^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; for multiple edges between <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, choose arbitrarily one of the arrows between <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,\rightarrow)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,\rightarrow)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us prove that <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(G^{\prime},\rightarrow^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G^{\prime},\rightarrow^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> forms a two-acyclic factorization system. As seen in the proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem40" title="Lemma 3.40. ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.40</span></a>, as <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(G^{\prime},\rightarrow^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G^{\prime},\rightarrow^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> does not have <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles we only need to look at condition <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem36" title="Definition 3.36 ([]). ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.36</span></a>. As <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are triangle-free, condition <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(iii)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(iii)</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be satisfied if there are any <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> arrows. This means that <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is obtained from <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,\rightarrow)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,\rightarrow)</annotation></semantics></math>, does not have regular arrows <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>. By definitions of <math id="S3.SS4.p17.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrel{\hbox to11.58pt{\vbox to4.28pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 0.09999pt\lower-2.13869pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke=#000000} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill=#000000} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width=0.4pt} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin=1} {
