∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Then the underlying undirected graph of <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)[X]</annotation></semantics></math>, which is triangle-free with chromatic number equals to <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, does not admit a two-acyclic factorization system by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem41" title="Proposition 3.41. ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.41</span></a>. By <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem42" title="Proposition 3.42. ‣ 3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.42</span></a>, any multigraph obtained from the underlying undirected graph of <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)[X]</annotation></semantics></math> by adding some edges between two elements of <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)[X]</annotation></semantics></math> already joined by an edge, does not admit either a two-acyclic factorization system. This proves that <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H[X]</annotation></semantics></math> is not a canonical join graph.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we prove <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> from the introduction, that gives a way to compute the dimension of a semidistributive extremal lattice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem43" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem43.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.43</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem43.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a poset. The <span id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">critical pairs</span> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> are pairs <math id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)\in P\times P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)\in P\times P</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> are incomparable, for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&lt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;a</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&lt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;b</annotation></semantics></math>, and for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&gt;b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&gt;b</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem43.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y&gt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem44" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem44.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.44</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem44.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem44.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a poset. The <span id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">graph of critical pairs</span> <math id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P)</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is the directed graph whose vertices are the critical pairs and edges are <math id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)\rightarrow(c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)\rightarrow(c,d)</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq c</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">critical pairs simplicial complex</span> <math id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(P)</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is the simplicial complex whose faces correspond to the subsets of critical pairs that induce an acyclic subgraph of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem44.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P)</annotation></semantics></math> (we mean that we need to avoid directed cycles).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cover set</em> of a simplicial complex <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of the faces of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> such that every vertex of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in a face of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem45" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem45.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.45</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem45.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, Proposition 14 and the discussion that follows]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem45.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem45.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem45.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem45.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any poset <math id="S3.Thmtheorem45.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, the dimension of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem45.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the minimum size of a cover set of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem45.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(P)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following is easy to prove.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem46" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem46.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.46</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem46.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem46.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem46.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem46.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem46.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a semidistributive lattice. Then the critical pairs are exactly the pairs <math id="S3.Thmtheorem46.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,\kappa(j))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,\kappa(j))</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem46.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem46.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j_{*}\not\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>MIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j_{*}\not\in\mathrm{MIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, which means <math id="S3.Thmtheorem46.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(j)\neq j_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(j)\neq j_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, for <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> a semidistributive lattice, we identify a critical pair with the join-irreducible element given by its first coordinate. Then the graph <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph on vertices the join-irreducible elements <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(j)\neq j_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(j)\neq j_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, with an edge <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(i)\geq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(i)\geq j</annotation></semantics></math>. Looking at <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Proposition 2.9. ‣ 2.3. Semidistributive lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>, when <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a semidistributive extremal lattice, we see that <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math> and the Galois graph <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> are similar. We make this observation precise in the next result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A 2-cycle on two vertices <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> in a directed graph will be represented by <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\longleftrightarrow y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟷</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\longleftrightarrow y</annotation></semantics></math>. See <a href="#S3.F6" title="In 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, which is partly taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, Figures 1 and 2]</cite>, for an illustration of the following result in a case where there are no <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(j)=j_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(j)=j_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
