<div id="S3.Thmtheorem47" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem47.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.47</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem47.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a semidistributive extremal lattice.
The graph of critical pairs <math id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from the Galois graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> by adding a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle between all pairs of join-irreducible elements <math id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> that form an independent set of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, and then removing the vertices <math id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem47.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(j)=j_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(j)=j_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us prove that if <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(i)\neq i_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(i)\neq i_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(j)\neq j_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(j)\neq j_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\rightarrow i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\rightarrow i</annotation></semantics></math> is not in <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. This follows from the definitions of the arrows in each graph; <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math> means <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(i)\geq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(i)\geq j</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Proposition 2.9. ‣ 2.3. Semidistributive lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\rightarrow i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\rightarrow i</annotation></semantics></math> is not in <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> means <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq\kappa(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq\kappa(i)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus if we have <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles in <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> they disappear in <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, but since <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 2.4 ([, Theorem 11]). ‣ 2.2. Extremal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> does not have <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles. The Lemma follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem48" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem48.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.48</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem48.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem48.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem48.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem48.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem48.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a semidistributive extremal lattice. The isolated vertices of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem48.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> are the join-irreducible elements <math id="S3.Thmtheorem48.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem48.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(i)=i_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(i)=i_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(i)=i_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(i)=i_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us prove that <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is an isolated vertex of <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which means that for every <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}</annotation></semantics></math> is not an independent set. Seeking a contradiction, assume that <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent set of <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, which means <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq\kappa(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq\kappa(j)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leq\kappa(i)=i_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leq\kappa(i)=i_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;\kappa(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;\kappa(j)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is absurd.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(i)\neq i_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(i)\neq i_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. We want to prove that <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is not an isolated vertex of <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is semidistributive and extremal, by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 2.11 ([, Corollary 6.7]). ‣ 2.3. Semidistributive lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a> the downward label sets of <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> are the independence sets of its Galois graph <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. In a semidistributive lattice, the set of all downward label sets is the same as that of all upward label sets, which are denoted by <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for an element <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in L</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Corollary 6.4]</cite>. By definition of <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{J}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in U(i_{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in U(i_{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(i)\neq i_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(i)\neq i_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|U(i_{*})|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|U(i_{*})|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is part of an independence set of size bigger than two, which proves that it is not an isolated vertex of <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> a semidistributive extremal lattice, we denote by <math id="S3.SS5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(L)</annotation></semantics></math> the undirected graph formed by the subgraph of <math id="S3.SS5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by keeping only the <math id="S3.SS5.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles <math id="S3.SS5.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\longleftrightarrow y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟷</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\longleftrightarrow y</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS5.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math> and replacing these by an edge joining <math id="S3.SS5.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S3.SS5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">null graph</em> is the graph that does not have vertices. An <em id="S3.SS5.p11.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">empty graph</em> is a graph without edges (but it can have vertices).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem49" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem49.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.49</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem49.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem49.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem49.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem49.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem49.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a semidistributive extremal lattice. Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem49.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of the graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem49.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(L)</annotation></semantics></math> with the empty graph whose vertices are the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem49.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem49.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(i)=i_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(i)=i_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem48" title="Lemma 3.48. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.48</span></a>, we know that the isolated vertices of <math id="S3.SS5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> are the join-irreducible elements <math id="S3.SS5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(i)=i_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(i)=i_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the proposition follows from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem47" title="Lemma 3.47. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.47</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem50" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem50.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.50</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem50.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem50.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem50.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem50.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem50.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a semidistributive extremal lattice. The cover sets of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem50.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(L)</annotation></semantics></math> are the partitions of the vertices of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem50.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(L)</annotation></semantics></math> into independent sets.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">By definition the vertices of <math id="S3.SS5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(L)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(L)</annotation></semantics></math> are the same.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a cover set of <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. We know that <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a partition of the vertices of <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we want to prove that it is a partition into independent sets. Let <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. By hypothesis <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a face of <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(L)</annotation></semantics></math>, which means that the induced subgraph of <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> does not have directed cycles. In particular it does not contain <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles, which proves that <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent set of <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, let <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a partition of <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(L)</annotation></semantics></math> into independent sets. We know that it is a partition of the vertices of <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. By hypothesis <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles. But by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem47" title="Lemma 3.47. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.47</span></a> and the fact that <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> does not have directed cycles (<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Proposition 2.4 ([, Theorem 11]). ‣ 2.2. Extremal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>), it follows that the only directed cycles in <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-cycles. Thus the induced subgraph of <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(L)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain directed cycles, which proves that <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a face of <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a cover set of <math id="S3.SS5.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p16.1" class="ltx_p">We can now prove the main result of this section (which is <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> from the introduction):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem51" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem51.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.51</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem51.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem51.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem51.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem51.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem51.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a semidistributive extremal lattice with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem51.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|L|&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|L|&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem51.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(L)~=~\chi(\overline{G(L)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(L)~=~\chi(\overline{G(L)})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.SS5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a chain with at least two elements, then the underlying undirected graph of <math id="S3.SS5.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete graph on <math id="S3.SS5.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|L|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|L|-1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. Thus <math id="S3.SS5.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> is an empty graph with at least one vertex, and the result follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.SS5.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is not a chain, then the underlying unidrected graph of <math id="S3.SS5.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is not the complete graph. Thus, by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem49" title="Proposition 3.49. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.49</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS5.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is not the null graph and <math id="S3.SS5.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(K(L))=\chi(\overline{G(L)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(K(L))=\chi(\overline{G(L)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we conclude using <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem45" title="Theorem 3.45 ([, Proposition 14 and the discussion that follows]). ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorems</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.45</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem50" title="Proposition 3.50. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.50</span></a> which together gives <math id="S3.SS5.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(K(L))=\dim(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(K(L))=\dim(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem52" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem52.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.52</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem52.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem52.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem52.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We defined the Galois graph for any extremal lattice <math id="S3.Thmtheorem52.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, without the assumption that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem52.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is semidistributive. The lattice <math id="S3.Thmtheorem52.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S3.F7" title="In 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows that it is necessary to have the semidistributivity in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem51" title="Theorem 3.51. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.51</span></a>. Indeed, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem52.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is extremal but not semidistributive, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem52.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(L)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(L)=3</annotation></semantics></math> and the complement of its Galois graph is a path on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem52.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, thus <math id="S3.Thmtheorem52.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\overline{G(L)})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\overline{G(L)})=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem53" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem53.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.53</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem53.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem53.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem53.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem53.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem53.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem53.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a distributive lattice. Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem53.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(L)=\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>width</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(L)=\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(L))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the fundamental theorem of finite distributive lattices, the Galois graph <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the subposet <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, we have an edge <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\rightarrow j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\rightarrow j</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq j</annotation></semantics></math> in the subposet <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the incomparability graph of <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{JIrr}(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{JIrr}(L)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Dilworth’s decomposition theorem, the chromatic number of this latter graph equals the maximum size of an antichain, which is <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(L))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>width</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(L))</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude using <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem51" title="Theorem 3.51. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.51</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p20.1" class="ltx_p">For a finite simple graph <math id="S3.SS5.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote by <math id="S3.SS5.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(G)</annotation></semantics></math> its clique number and by <math id="S3.SS5.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(G)</annotation></semantics></math> its maximum degree. The following is well-known and easy:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem54" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem54.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.54</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem54.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem54.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem54.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem54.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any simple graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem54.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem54.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, we have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem54.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(G)\leq\chi(G)\leq d(G)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(G)\leq\chi(G)\leq d(G)+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p21.1" class="ltx_p">The number of independent sets of <math id="S3.SS5.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of elements of <math id="S3.SS5.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Corollary 5.6]</cite>). This corresponds to the number of cliques of <math id="S3.SS5.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 2.11 ([, Corollary 6.7]). ‣ 2.3. Semidistributive lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a>, a clique of <math id="S3.SS5.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the downward label set, or upward label set using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Corollary 6.4]</cite>, of some element of <math id="S3.SS5.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS5.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(\overline{G(L)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\overline{G(L)})</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum number of elements that cover, or are covered, by some vertex of <math id="S3.SS5.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a lower bound for the dimension of <math id="S3.SS5.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem51" title="Theorem 3.51. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.51</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem54" title="Lemma 3.54. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.54</span></a>. We obtained differently this result in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem21" title="Lemma 2.21. ‣ 2.7. The order dimension of a poset ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.21</span></a>, proving it is true for any semidistributive lattice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Applications of our dimension result</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we use <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem51" title="Theorem 3.51. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.51</span></a> to obtain the dimension of some semidistributive extremal lattices that appeared recently in algebraic combinatorics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>The Hochschild and bubble lattices</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection we determine the dimension of the Hochschild lattice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> and of one of its generalizations called the bubble lattices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. The Hochschild lattice is obtained as an orientation of the 1-skeleton of the Hochschild polytopes which appeared in algebraic topology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the discussion on extremal lattices from <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. Extremal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, in particular <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 2.5 ([]). ‣ 2.2. Extremal lattices ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, which enable us to define an extremal lattice by giving its Galois graph.</p>
</div>
