<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hochschild lattice</span> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Hoch}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hoch</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Hoch}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is an extremal lattice whose Galois graph is isomorphic to the directed graph whose vertices are <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{(i,j)\mid i\in\{1,2\},\,i\leq j\leq n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(i,j)\mid i\in\{1,2\},\,i\leq j\leq n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the edges are <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\rightarrow(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\rightarrow(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)\neq(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)\neq(i^{\prime},j^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and either we have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, or we have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=i^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=i^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We give in <a href="#S4.F8.sf1" title="In Figure 8 ‣ 4.1. The Hochschild and bubble lattices ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(a)</span></a> the Galois graph of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Hoch}(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hoch</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Hoch}(3)</annotation></semantics></math>, and we give this lattice in <a href="#S4.F9" title="In 4.1. The Hochschild and bubble lattices ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> (this figure is taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, Proposition 4.27]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{x_{1},\dots,x_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\{y_{1},\dots,y_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\{y_{1},\dots,y_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be two disjoint linearly ordered alphabets.
For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bubble lattice</span> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is an extremal lattice whose Galois graph is the directed graph whose vertices are <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sqcup Y\sqcup(X\times Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊔</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sqcup Y\sqcup(X\times Y)</annotation></semantics></math> and the edges are <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}\rightarrow l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}\rightarrow l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}\neq l_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}\neq l_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and either:</p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}=(x_{s},y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}=(x_{s},y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}=x_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}=x_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, or</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}=y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}=y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}=(x_{s},y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}=(x_{s},y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, or</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S4.I1.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}=(x_{s},y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}=(x_{s},y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}=(x_{s^{\prime}},y_{t^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}=(x_{s^{\prime}},y_{t^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geq s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geq s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leq t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leq t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, Proposition 4.19]</cite> that <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Bub}(n-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Bub}(n-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Hoch}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hoch</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Hoch}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, these lattices are semidistributive and extremal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now we use <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem51" title="Theorem 3.51. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.51</span></a> to obtain the dimension of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Hoch}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hoch</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Hoch}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Hoch}(n))=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Hoch</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Hoch}(n))=n</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">From the Galois graph of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Hoch}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hoch</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Hoch}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> given in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 4.1 ([, Theorem 1.1]). ‣ 4.1. The Hochschild and bubble lattices ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we can deduce easily <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:=\{(1,i)\mid i\in[n]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:=\{(1,i)\mid i\in[n]\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B:=\{(2,i)\mid 2\leq i\leq n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B:=\{(2,i)\mid 2\leq i\leq n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph whose vertices are <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\sqcup B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊔</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\sqcup B</annotation></semantics></math>, and it can be described by the fact that the induced subgraph on <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an empty graph, and for every <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> the induced subgraph on <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(1,i)\}\sqcup B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(1,i)\}\sqcup B</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete graph on <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. With the latter we obtain <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\overline{G(L)})\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\overline{G(L)})\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, by coloring the vertices of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(1,1)\}\sqcup B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(1,1)\}\sqcup B</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> different colors, and coloring the vertices of <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> all with the same color as <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, this gives a proper coloring with <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> colors. Thus <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\overline{G(L)})=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\overline{G(L)})=n</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies the proposition, by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem51" title="Theorem 3.51. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.51</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, we can directly obtain the dimension of the Bubble lattices (which is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, Conjecture 4.24]</cite>) using the bounds on dimension from <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem20" title="Proposition 2.20 ([, Theorem 6]). ‣ 2.7. The order dimension of a poset ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.20</span></a>, which also implies <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4.1. The Hochschild and bubble lattices ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))=m+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Bub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))=m+n</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, Corollary 4.10]</cite>, it is proved that the subposet <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{JIrr}(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Bub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{JIrr}(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))</annotation></semantics></math> on the join-irreducible elements of <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of an <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-element antichain with <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> chains. This proves that <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+n</annotation></semantics></math> atoms and <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(L))=m+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>width</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(L))=m+n</annotation></semantics></math>, which is an upper-bound of <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Bub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem20" title="Proposition 2.20 ([, Theorem 6]). ‣ 2.7. The order dimension of a poset ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.20</span></a>. Since <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is a semidistributive lattice with <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+n</annotation></semantics></math> atoms, using <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem21" title="Lemma 2.21. ‣ 2.7. The order dimension of a poset ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.21</span></a> we have <math id="S4.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))\geq m+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Bub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Bub}(m,n))\geq m+n</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies the proposition.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>The <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-word lattices</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus in this section on another generalization of the Hochschild lattice, called <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-word lattices (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, Definition 77]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, Definition 77]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-words are the words <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=w_{1}\dots w_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=w_{1}\dots w_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> on the alphabet <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1,\dots,m+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1,\dots,m+1\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}\neq m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}\neq m+1</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in[m]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}=s</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{j}\geq s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{j}\geq s</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-word poset</span> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the poset on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-words with the product order, which means <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\leq w^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\leq w^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}\leq w^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}\leq w^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The poset <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is a lattice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, Corollary 84]</cite> and is semidistributive and extremal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>. Similarly to the Bubble lattice <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bub</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Bub}(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>, it is isomorphic to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Hoch}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hoch</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Hoch}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, Example 78]</cite>. But these are different generalizations of the Hochschild lattice. See <a href="#S4.F8.sf2" title="In Figure 8 ‣ 4.1. The Hochschild and bubble lattices ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8(b)</span></a> for the Galois graph of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(2,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <a href="#S4.F10" title="In 4.1. The Hochschild and bubble lattices ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> for the lattice <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(2,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, Lemma 12]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The join-irreducible elements of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-words <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(i,j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(i,j)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[m]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{2,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{2,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{(i,j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{(i,j)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-word with the first <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> letters equal to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and other letters are <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>-word whose <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> letter is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+1</annotation></semantics></math> and all other letters are <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, Proposition 22]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The downward label set of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in W(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in W(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a^{(i,j)}\mid\text{$i$ is the position of the rightmost letter $j$ in $w$, where $j\in[m]$}\}\sqcup\{b^{(i)}\mid w_{i}=m+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> is the position of the rightmost letter </mtext><mi>j</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> in </mtext><mi>w</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">, where </mtext><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a^{(i,j)}\mid\text{$i$ is the position of the rightmost letter $j$ in $w$, where $j\in[m]$}\}\sqcup\{b^{(i)}\mid w_{i}=m+1\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(W(m,n))=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(W(m,n))=n</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L:=W(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L:=W(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem51" title="Theorem 3.51. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.51</span></a>, we have <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(L)=\chi(\overline{G(L)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(L)=\chi(\overline{G(L)})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the chromatic number of the canonical join graph of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Color a join-irreducible element of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> by the position of its rightmost non-zero letter. By <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 4.7 ([, Proposition 22]). ‣ 4.2. The (𝑚,𝑛)-word lattices ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>, this is a proper coloring with <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> colors of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(L)}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(L)\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(L)\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. As the downward label set of the maximum element <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(m+1)\dots(m+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(m+1)\dots(m+1)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> elements, thus <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\overline{G(L)})\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\overline{G(L)})\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>, and the proposition follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We saw that the bounds from <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem20" title="Proposition 2.20 ([, Theorem 6]). ‣ 2.7. The order dimension of a poset ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.20</span></a> were enough to find the dimension of the bubble lattices. For <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math> it is not enough as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, Lemma 24]</cite> gives its subposet on its join-irreducible elements and it follows that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(W(m,n))=n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>width</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>JIrr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{width}(\mathrm{JIrr}(W(m,n))=n+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is strictly bigger than <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(W(m,n))=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(W(m,n))=n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>The parabolic Tamari lattice</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we are interested in the parabolic Tamari lattice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, which is a generalization of the Tamari lattice.</p>
</div>
