<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>. </span><span id="S4.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The parabolic Tamari lattice <math id="S4.F12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tam}(1,2,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tam}(1,2,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer composition of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We draw <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> horizontally aligned nodes labeled <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math> and put vertical bars separating the node <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from the node <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{i}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{i}+1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math>. The sets of nodes between two such separating bars are the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-regions, there are naturally labeled <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\dots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\dots,k</annotation></semantics></math> from left to right.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arc is a pair <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq a&lt;b\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq a&lt;b\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> are in different <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-regions. We represent graphically an <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arc <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> by drawing a curve leaving the bottom of node <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, staying below the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-region containing <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, moving up and above the following <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-regions until it reaches the top of node <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. We will also denote an <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arc <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Two <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arcs <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},b_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},b_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{2},b_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{2},b_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compatible</em> if <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\neq a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\neq a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}\neq b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}\neq b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and if <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}&lt;a_{2}&lt;b_{1}&lt;b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}&lt;a_{2}&lt;b_{1}&lt;b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> then either <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2},b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2},b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are in the same <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-region, and if <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}&lt;a_{2}&lt;b_{2}&lt;b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}&lt;a_{2}&lt;b_{2}&lt;b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are in the same <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-region. A set of pairwise compatible <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arcs are called <em id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">noncrossing <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-partitions</em>. The set of all noncrossing <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-partitions is denoted by <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Nonc}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Nonc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Nonc}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. See <a href="#S4.F11" title="In 4.3. The parabolic Tamari lattice ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> (which is taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>) for an illustration of a noncrossing <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-partition, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-regions are depicted with different colors. In this figure <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=(3,4,2,1,4,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=(3,4,2,1,4,2)</annotation></semantics></math> and the two red <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arcs are <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,5)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(5,13)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(5,13)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, Theorem 1.3]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer composition of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">parabolic Tamari lattice</span> <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is an extremal lattice whose Galois graph is isomorphic to the directed graph whose vertices are the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arcs <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{a,b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{a,b}</annotation></semantics></math> and the edges are between different <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arcs <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{a_{1},b_{1}}\rightarrow w_{a_{2},b_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{a_{1},b_{1}}\rightarrow w_{a_{2},b_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that either <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are in the same <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-region and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\leq a_{2}&lt;b_{2}\leq b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\leq a_{2}&lt;b_{2}\leq b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are in different <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-regions and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}&lt;a_{1}&lt;b_{2}\leq b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}&lt;a_{1}&lt;b_{2}\leq b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are also in different <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-regions.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">See <a href="#S4.F12" title="In 4.3. The parabolic Tamari lattice ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> for an example.
This lattice was defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. The fact that they are semidistributive and extremal is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>. If <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=(1,1,\dots,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=(1,1,\dots,1)</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Tamari lattice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, Proposition 8]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, Proposition 3.5]</cite>, it follows that the canonical join graph of <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))}</annotation></semantics></math>, is the graph whose vertices are the <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arcs, with an edge between two compatible such arcs. Thus its cliques are the noncrossing <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-partitions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.11</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=(\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer composition of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> the biggest integer appearing in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))=n-\max(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))=n-\max(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}=\max(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}=\max(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>.
A proper coloring of <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-\max(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-\max(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> colors can be obtained by coloring an <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arc <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is in an <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-region labeled by <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>, and the other <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arcs <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> which have not yet been assigned a color are color by <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the colors are labels of nodes that are not in the <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-region, thus this uses at most (in fact exactly) <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-\max(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-\max(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> colors. This is a proper coloring since two <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arcs having the same color share the same starting node, or ending node, thus they are not compatible. This proves that <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))})\leq n-\max(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))})\leq n-\max(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We still need to prove that <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(\overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))})\geq n-\max(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(\overline{G(\mathrm{Tam}(\alpha))})\geq n-\max(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. This comes from the fact that we can find a noncrossing <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-partition with this number of <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arcs; for every <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[k]</annotation></semantics></math> draw an <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arc between the <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> node of the <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-region and the <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> node of the <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i+1)^{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i+1)^{st}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-region whenever these nodes exist. This forms a noncrossing <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-partition with <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-\max(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-\max(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> number of <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-arcs.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For example, the planar lattice <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tam}(1,2,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tam</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tam}(1,2,1)</annotation></semantics></math> represented in <a href="#S4.F12" title="In 4.3. The parabolic Tamari lattice ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> is of dimension <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus in this section on the lattices of torsion classes of a gentle tree. These include any orientation of the path quiver <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus it contains the Cambrian lattice <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Camb}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Camb</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Camb}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> of type <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to a Coxeter element <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. When the path quiver is linearly oriented, the corresponding lattice is the Tamari lattice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.12</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For those only interested in the Cambrian lattices of type <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, a similar combinatorial model to the above discussion about the parabolic Tamari lattice exists in terms of noncrossing partitions (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, Example 4.9]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, section 8.3]</cite>). In this model, by coloring an arc with the label of its starting node, we prove analogously to <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem11" title="Proposition 4.11. ‣ 4.3. The parabolic Tamari lattice ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a> that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Camb_{c}})=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Camb</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Camb_{c}})=n</annotation></semantics></math> for all Coxeter element <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem17" title="Corollary 4.17. ‣ 4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.17</span></a> below.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.13</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, Theorem 2.10]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is representation finite and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mod}\,A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mod}\,A</annotation></semantics></math> has no cycles, then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tors}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Tors</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tors}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite semidistributive extremal lattice.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This proposition applies to the simply laced Dynkin quivers. Other examples are the gentle trees (see <a href="#S2" title="2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). We focus on the latter. To see more details about the combinatorics of gentle algebras, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a gentle tree <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an (almost) canonical way to draw it so that arrows always go horizontally to the right or vertically upwards, and such that the relations correspond to a change of direction. (It is almost canonical because for a given arrow, we can choose whether to draw it horizontally or vertically; this forces the direction of all other arrows). From now on, we assume that our gentle trees are drawn in this way (having fixed an orientation of the edges). The horizontal arrows can be thought as east steps and the vertical ones as north steps. Thus, the relations correspond to take an east step followed by a north step, or a north step followed by an east step. See <a href="#S4.F14.sf1" title="In Figure 14 ‣ 4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14(a)</span></a> for an example of a gentle tree drawn with our conventions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The indecomposable modules of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the
walks in the tree which take the unique shortest path in the underlying graph between their two endpoints without containing two steps that form a relation. Those are called the <em id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">string modules</em> and we write <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> for the indecomposable module corresponding to the walk <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. As a representation this corresponds to putting <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math> at every vertex visited by <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> at every other vertex and the identity map id between any two adjacent copies of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. With the orientation of the gentle tree fixed as above, there is a canonical way to read a walk <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>: from top left to bottom right. Each walk therefore has a well-defined top left endpoint, and a well-defined bottom right endpoint. We refer to them simply as the <em id="S4.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left endpoint</em> and the <em id="S4.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right endpoint</em>. Then, any walk <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> can be uniquely written as the word recording the arrows <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> or opposite arrows <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> met on the way from its left endpoint to its right endpoint.</p>
</div>
