<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.14</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be two different walks on a gentle tree <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> that share the same right endpoint. Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are not hom-orthogonal.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The walks <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma,\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma,\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, sharing the same right endpoint, can be written <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=\sigma_{1}\alpha\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\sigma_{1}\alpha\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}=\sigma^{\prime}_{1}\beta^{-1}\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}=\sigma^{\prime}_{1}\beta^{-1}\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\neq\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\neq\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover since the underlying undirected graph of <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree, we know that the walks <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have disjoint support. Without loss of generality, we can assume that <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is an east step and <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> a north step. Then we have a non-zero morphism from <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the identity map on the vertices of <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and the zero map on every other vertex. See <a href="#S4.F13" title="In 4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, with on the right the crucial part of this non-zero morphism.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.15</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, Theorem 1.8]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is representation finite, the faces of the canonical join complex are the semibricks.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Thus for <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> representation finite, <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(\mathrm{Tors}(A))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tors</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(\mathrm{Tors}(A))}</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph whose vertices are the bricks and we have an edge between <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if they are hom-orthogonal.</p>
</div>
