<div id="S4.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.16</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a gentle tree having <math id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Tors}(T))=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tors</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Tors}(T))=n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since a gentle tree is representation finite, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem15" title="Proposition 4.15 ([, Theorem 1.8]). ‣ 4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.15</span></a> applies. The category <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{mod}\,A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{mod}\,A</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> simples and they form a semibrick as their supports are disjoint. Thus <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Tors}(T))~\geq~n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tors</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Tors}(T))~\geq~n</annotation></semantics></math>. To conclude, let us prove that <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Tors}(T))\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tors</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Tors}(T))\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>. For this we find a proper coloring of <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(\mathrm{Tors}(T))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tors</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(\mathrm{Tors}(T))}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> colors. Since <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a gentle tree, every brick of <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a string representation <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus it has a right endpoint. Color a brick with the label of its right endpoint. This gives a coloring with <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> colors of <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{G(\mathrm{Tors}(T))}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Tors</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{G(\mathrm{Tors}(T))}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is proper by <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem14" title="Lemma 4.14. ‣ 4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.14</span></a>. This proves the proposition.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.17</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S4.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathrm{Camb_{c}})=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Camb</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathrm{Camb_{c}})=n</annotation></semantics></math> for any Coxeter element <math id="S4.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows from <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem16" title="Proposition 4.16. ‣ 4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.16</span></a> since <math id="S4.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Camb}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Camb</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Camb}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the lattice of torsion classes of an orientation (determined by <math id="S4.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>) of the path quiver <math id="S4.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a gentle tree having <math id="S4.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For example, <a href="#S4.F14.sf2" title="In Figure 14 ‣ 4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14(b)</span></a> is a Cambrian lattice of type <math id="S4.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus is of dimension <math id="S4.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Open questions</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We finish by giving some open questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Figure 9]</cite> an example is given of an extremal congruence uniform lattice of dimension 3 obtained from the doubling of a chain, thus of dimension 1, of an extremal congruence uniform lattice of dimension 2. This is a counterexample to the observation that often <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(L[C])=\dim(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(L[C])=\dim(L)</annotation></semantics></math> when the interval <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(C)&lt;\dim(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(C)&lt;\dim(L)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a lattice. For which condition on an interval <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> do we have <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(L[C])=\dim(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(L[C])=\dim(L)</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem26" title="Theorem 3.26. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.26</span></a>, we proved the equivalence between extremality and shellability in the case of congruence uniform lattices. For the more general case of semidistributive lattices, we know that extremality implies shellability (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Corollary 3.7 ([, Theorem 1.4]). ‣ 3.1. Edge-labellings and left modularity ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>). What about the converse?</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Is it true that any semidistributive shellable lattice is extremal?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Another direction to generalize <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem26" title="Theorem 3.26. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.26</span></a> is to look at join-congruence uniform and meet-congruence uniform lattices. We proved in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem25" title="Corollary 3.25. ‣ 3.3. Shellability of congruence normal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Corollary</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.25</span></a> that any join-congruence uniform lattice that is shellable is join-extremal. What about the converse:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Is it true that any join-congruence uniform lattice that is join-extremal is shellable?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Induced subgraphs of a canonical join graph ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> we proved that an induced subgraph of a canonical join graph is not always a canonical join graph, even if the initial canonical join graph can be obtained from a semidistributive lattice that is extremal. For similar questions, it would be nice to have an affirmative answer to the following question, as it would restrict the class of lattices we have to look at:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be the canonical join graph of a semidistributive lattice. Is it true that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can also be obtained as the canonical join graph of an extremal semidistributive lattice?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Different lattices that we considered in this paper can be obtained as the orientation of the 1-skeleton of a polytope by choosing a generic cost vector (see the precise definition in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>). In our examples, all the ones coming from a <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polytope were of order dimension <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Since in these examples those lattices are all semidistributive we know that we have the lower bound <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> for their order dimension by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem21" title="Lemma 2.21. ‣ 2.7. The order dimension of a poset ‣ 2. Background ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.21</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If a lattice <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the 1-skeleton of a <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polytope, is it true that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(L)=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(L)=n</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Question 5.5. ‣ 5. Open questions ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Question</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> is false, does it hold with the extra hypothesis that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is semidistributive and/or extremal?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Note that it seems that the face lattice associate to a polytope behaves very differently since for any <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> there exist <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polytopes whose face lattice has arbitrarily large order dimension, and for <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math> the face lattice has order dimension less or equal to 4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The lattices of torsion classes are <span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hasse-regular</span> lattices, meaning any element <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in their Hasse diagram has the same degree, which is the sum of the number of elements <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> covers with the number of elements that cover <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. A lattice is called <em id="S5.p8.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-Hasse-regular</em> if the degree of any element is <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.7</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Is it true that any semidistributive <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-Hasse-regular lattice is of dimension <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>? If not, is it true if the lattice is additionally congruence uniform?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We recalled that the lattice of torsion classes of simply laced Dynkin types are semidistributive and extremal (<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem13" title="Proposition 4.13 ([, Theorem 2.10]). ‣ 4.4. The lattice of torsion classes of a gentle tree ‣ 4. Applications of our dimension result ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.13</span></a>). Thus we could try to use <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem51" title="Theorem 3.51. ‣ 3.5. The dimension of semidistributive extremal lattices ‣ 3. The main results ‣ Extremality in semidistributive lattices" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.51</span></a> to answer the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 5.8</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What is the dimension of the Cambrian lattices of types <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{E}</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
